+// The persona a launch composes when --fingerprint-platform named none.
+//
+// Not the host's own platform, which is what this used to be. The claimed OS is
+// the axis a user is actually choosing, and the honest default per host is not
+// uniform:
+//
+//   macos host    -> macos persona. The safe case, and coherent by
+//                    construction: a macOS host can natively serve any claimed
+//                    macOS renderer, so identity and capability agree without
+//                    anything having to be bridged.
+//   windows host  -> windows persona. Same argument.
+//   linux host    -> windows persona. This is the deliberate cross-OS default.
+//                    A Linux host is the deployment target and a Windows
+//                    persona is what most launches want, so composing Linux
+//                    there would be answering a question nobody asked. It is
+//                    the least bad cross-OS pairing -- largely a font question,
+//                    which the user can fix by provisioning the Windows set --
+//                    and it is still a risk, so it is recorded as a limitation
+//                    rather than left silent, and --fingerprint-platform=linux
+//                    overrides it.
+//
+// An unrecognised or empty host platform maps to nothing, so the caller's
+// IsKnownPlatform check refuses the launch exactly as it did before: composing
+// a persona for a platform with no corpus behind it would be worse than
+// inheriting the host.
+std::string_view DefaultPersonaForHost(std::string_view host_platform) {
+  if (host_platform == "macos") {
+    return "macos";
+  }
+  if (host_platform == "windows" || host_platform == "linux") {
+    return "windows";
+  }
+  return host_platform;
+}
