 // seed would silently compose an identity when pass-through was asked for.
 inline constexpr char kFingerprintHostValue[] = "host";
 
-// Platform persona: windows | macos | linux. Defaults to the host's.
+// Platform persona: windows | macos | linux.
 //
-// This does not move the GPU cluster. Across graphics backends nothing
-// transfers -- the same NVIDIA silicon produces a different capability digest
-// through D3D11 than through Vulkan -- so the anchor is always selected from
-// what the host can actually serve, and a persona/cluster mismatch is reported
-// as a limitation instead of hidden.
+// The default is host-conditional and is not always the host's own: a macOS
+// host composes macos, a Windows host composes windows, and a Linux host
+// composes WINDOWS. The last is deliberate -- a Linux server is the deployment
+// target and a Windows persona is what most launches there want -- and it is
+// the way out of it as well: --fingerprint-platform=linux composes the host's
+// own OS.
+//
+// This selects the GPU cluster. The anchor is drawn from the anchors captured
+// on the claimed platform, so a macos persona carries a Metal cluster, a
+// windows persona a D3D11 one and a linux persona a Vulkan one, whatever the
+// host's own graphics backend happens to be. Within a platform the catalogue
+// has exactly one backend, so choosing the persona determines the backend and
+// there is nothing left for the host's to decide.
+//
+// A cross-OS persona costs what a fork cannot reach: installed fonts stay the
+// host's. That is recorded as a composition limitation, which
+// --fingerprint-explain prints, rather than refused.
 inline constexpr char kFingerprintPlatform[] = "fingerprint-platform";
 
 // Pin the GPU anchor by id instead of drawing one.
